rtial arts  like tai chi taekwondo and yes most  relevantly wing chun yen spent months  training to prepare for this role when  he's described as the most emotionally  and mentally difficult in his career but  it paid off because it man was a  critical and commercial success and  spawned a four film series grandmaster  man would have been proud  fae hung vs jon drunken master 2.  jackie chan is one of the all-time  greats and we could spend entire  episodes just discussing some of his  classic fights and stunts and maybe  we'll get to do that eventually but for  today we're focusing on one particularly  iconic battle fae hung versus jon from  the action comedy drunken master 2. jan  plays wang fei hung a legendary chinese  martial artist who can't seem to stay  out of trouble and yes as the movie's  title suggests he's got a bit of a  drinking problem okay so he's got a  serious drinking problem which leads him  to consistently get into fights and  almost causing his father to lose his  land  you'd think that the constant drama  would be enough to motivate faehun to  lay off the sauce but it ain't so simple  the only way faehung is able to unlock  the drunken boxing fighting style and  reach peak martial arts performance is  to be intoxicated  god  [Music]  though the film takes the idea of  drunken boxing quite literally there's  actually a style of chinese martial arts  that imitates the movements of a drunk  person one that leans heavily on  deception and strange striking angles  this technique is utilized by chan as he  fights real-life taekwondo champion ken  lo in this beautifully choreographed  expertly shot sequence both chan and low  are performing all their own stunts  i don't know what's more impressive  lowe's kicking abilities are chance  tolerance for pain that shot where john  kicks fae hung onto a pile of burning  red hot coals and then fei hung shuffles  and crawls across them yeah it looks  realistic doesn't it because it's real  chad actually crawled over hot coals and  did it twice because he didn't feel like  he nailed it on the first take the guys  has a burning desire for perfection  neo vs morpheus the matrix  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  if only learning kung fu was as simple  as hitting a download button like in the  neo vs morpheus fight from the matrix  when the wachowskis were conceptualizing  their action scenes they took direct  inspiration from several martial arts  films such as iron monkey once upon a  time in china and even drunken master  but the film that had the most obvious  influence was fist of legend a 1994 hong  kong actioner produced by and starring  jet li  fist of legend was a remake of the 1972  bruce lee film pistol fury we'll get to  that later if you're looking for clues  as to why the fighting in the matrix so  closely resembles the fighting in fist  of legend look no further than hong kong  martial arts choreographer yen woo ping  who the wachowskis were huge fans of and  imported to hollywood to work on their  films wu peng had only four months to  train and prep the matrix actors so they  could perform most of their own stunts  according to wu ping martial arts takes  a lifetime to master so they were in  quite a hurry adding that everyone had  injuries from the intensive process and  that it was all created with blood sweat  and tears  [Music]  come on stop trying to hit me and hit me  as they say no pain no gain and all that  work resulted in one of the most  groundbreaking fight scenes in cinematic  history in which a sparring program set  in a traditional east asian dojo is  constructed so that neo can practice his  hand-to-hand combat on morpheus this  sequence employs the tactics of wire foo  which is common in hong kong cinema and  combines kung fu with wire work hence  the name it's a beautifully shot tribute  to the films that inspired the  wachowskis and a testament to keanu  reeves lawrence fishburne and wu ping's  dedication to their work  rama vs machete gang 